# RainCore

**English** | [Русский](README.ru.md)

A modular C# library for building 3D games on top of OpenTK. It is split into separate assemblies (graphics, audio, core game logic, scenes, scripting, UI, turn-based battles) that you reference from your own game and use as much or as little of as you need. It is aimed at atmospheric, low-fi games: PS1/PS2-style rendering, dreamcore and backrooms moods, fog, dithering, vertex snapping.

On top of the libraries there are two ready-made tools: an editor for building scenes and a player for running projects. They are optional, the libraries work without them.

## What's inside

### Libraries

- **Core** (`RainCore.Core`): events, triggers, entities, moods, cameras, level contracts.
- **Events**: an RPG Maker-style system of event commands, trigger zones, pages and flag-based conditions. NPCs, posters, interactables, quests, dialogue.
- **Lua scripting** (`RainCore.Scripting`, MoonSharp): for logic that is too complex for event commands. A controlled API, no `io` / `os`. Event commands remain, Lua is an addition, not a replacement.
- **Mood system**: mood zones and mood events that change the atmosphere as the player moves.
- **Levels**: two implementations behind a common `ILevel` interface, a voxel world (`RainCore.Voxel`) and a mesh room, PS1/PS2 style (`RainCore.Scene`).
- **Graphics, Audio, UI**: OpenGL rendering through OpenTK, OpenAL audio with Ogg support, in-game UI and text.
- **Game modules**: `IGameModule` lets your project ship its own gameplay code as a separate assembly (see `RainCore.SampleModules`).
- **Turn-based** core for JRPG-style battles (`RainCore.TurnBased`).

### Tools

- **Editor** (`RainCore.EditorApp`, ImGui.NET) in the spirit of Unity / GameMaker:
  - scene tabs, Scene Manager, Hierarchy / Inspector / Project panels
  - gizmos (Move / Rotate / Scale), snapping, multi-select, Duplicate, prefabs
  - shared Undo/Redo with a History window
  - fly camera, Focus / Frame All, camera bookmarks
  - viewport lighting modes (Scene / Editor / Unlit / Wireframe)
  - Environment window: fog, ambient, exposure, PSX vertex snap and dither, 11 presets
  - built-in Lua script editor with syntax check
  - Play Scene / Play Project / Play From Here, changes are reverted on Stop
  - Console and Diagnostics (missing Player Start, broken models, Lua errors, and so on)
  - command palette (Ctrl+Shift+P) and search (Ctrl+K)
  - autosave recovery for unsaved scenes
  - **Build**: `dotnet publish` of the Player (self-contained, single file) plus a copy of the project content
- **Player** (`RainCore.Player`): runs a project, both from the editor and as a standalone build.

## Using it as a library

Add the assemblies you need to your game as project references:

```xml
<ItemGroup>
  <ProjectReference Include="..\RainCore\RainCore.Core\RainCore.Core.csproj" />
  <ProjectReference Include="..\RainCore\RainCore.Scripting\RainCore.Scripting.csproj" />
</ItemGroup>
```

The namespace is `RainCore`. `RainCore.Graphics`, `RainCore.Audio` and `RainCore.TurnBased` have almost no dependencies on the rest and can be used on their own.

## Structure

| Project | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `RainCore.Graphics` | Low-level rendering (OpenGL via OpenTK) |
| `RainCore.Audio` | Audio (OpenAL, NVorbis) |
| `RainCore.Core` | Events, triggers, entities, moods, cameras, level contracts |
| `RainCore.Voxel` | Voxel world |
| `RainCore.Scene` | Mesh / GLB scenes |
| `RainCore.UI` | In-game UI, text rendering |
| `RainCore.Project` | Project format and loading |
| `RainCore.Scripting` | Lua (MoonSharp) |
| `RainCore.TurnBased` | Turn-based battle logic |
| `RainCore.EditorApp` | The editor |
| `RainCore.Player` | The game runner |
| `RainCore.SampleModules` | Example game module (day/night pilot) |

## Requirements

- .NET 8 SDK
- A GPU with OpenGL support
- The editor currently targets `net8.0-windows` (some dialogs are Windows-only). The Player targets plain `net8.0`.

## Getting started

```bash
git clone <this-repo>
cd RainCore
dotnet build RainCore.sln

# editor
dotnet run --project RainCore.EditorApp

# player (pass a project folder, or run without arguments to use ./Project)
dotnet run --project RainCore.Player -- path/to/project
```

Create or open a project in the editor, add a few walls and a floor, place a Player Start, and press Play.

## What's not done

What is missing right now:

- Not implemented: subscenes, Scene Flow, visual Event Editor graph, Material Editor, animation editor, profiler, hot reload, key rebinding, project templates, light and camera objects.
- Prefabs are copies, not linked instances. Editing a prefab file does not update objects already placed.
- Script editor has no syntax highlighting or autocomplete.
- The ghost visual for mood events is only logged for now.
- The editor's file dialog is Windows-only.
- No multiplayer.


